<p>One of the benefits to being part of the Apple ecosystem is the plethora of apps that work across <em>all</em> of Apple’s devices so that you can access them whether you’re sitting in front of a Mac, out and about with your iPhone or even strapping on a Vision Pro headset. Come on — somebody out there has to be wearing one of those things.</p>
<p>This week’s look at interesting apps features four such multiplatform wonders that let you do everything from type with your voice, post on a social network or jot down random thoughts on at least two different Apple devices. And our lone macOS-only app should prove pretty useful in its own right.</p>
<p>If you’ve come across an app that you find useful no matter what device it works on, let us hear about it at <code>apps@sixcolors.com</code>.</p>
<h2>Voice Keyboard Pro</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/voice-keyboard-pro-mac1-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Screenshot of Voice Keyboard Pro showing a Quarterly Performance Review meeting: summary, discussion points, transcript option, email to jhrw9my2hv@privaterelay.appleid.com, auto-sending in 42s, buttons below." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>My high school typing teacher would frown upon hearing this, but typing sometimes feels like it’s more trouble than it’s worth. And since I’m the type of person who tends to speak aloud the words as I type them, why am I even bothering with the middleman of a keyboard and not just letting my voice handle all the work?</p>
<p><a href="https://voicekeyboardpro.com">Voice Keyboard Pro</a> lets me do just that, allowing me to press and hold a key on my MacBook Air keyboard — the Control key, by default, to be exact — and dictate into any app that allows for text entry. I’ve used Voice Keyboard Pro to enter text into BBEdit, the built-in Notes app, and the Gmail web page in Safari, all without any fuss.</p>
<p>Transcription is fairly accurate — Voice Keyboard Pro knew enough to properly capitalize “BBEdit,” for example. But it’s not flawless, as my efforts to dictate a message to Jason Snell kept turning his first name into “JSON.” I imagine that’s something the app would learn to correct over time.</p>
<p>Voice Keyboard Pro isn’t just about dictation, as the app offers a Meeting mode that not only records what’s said in one-on-ones, standup sessions and sprint planning, but also offers a nice summary of what you talk about afterwards.</p>
<p>You can try out Voice Keyboard Pro for free on a limited basis with five transcriptions per day. A $4.99/month Pro subscription unlocks the app and adds custom vocabulary and offline modes.</p>
<h2>Play</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/play-2-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="macOS app showing a video list with titles like 'Ex-Men: Gambit' and 'Easy Lentil &amp; Sausage Stew'. Right panel displays 'Booze On The Rocks' details: AI description, added 9/2/26, duration 3:55." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>I cannot tell you how many times this scenario plays out: I will be scrolling through my YouTube feed when I see a video and think “My wife will probably enjoy watching this, I’ll have to remember it for later.” Of course, I will not because my brain is too crowded to remember random YouTube videos upon command.</p>
<p>The simply-named app <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/play-save-videos-watch-later/id1596506190">Play</a> now does the remembering for me. Copy a YouTube link into Play, and it’s kept in a watchlist complete with metadata — you can even watch YouTube videos directly in the app. In addition to YouTube, Play also supports Vimeo, TikTok and Instagram Reels; I’ve also posted Bluesky links with embedded video into Play, though I have to click through to the website to play back the video.</p>
<p>Play has a tagging feature that helps you organize videos. For instance, I can tag videos containing recipes with a Cooking tag for easy reference later on. Other bookmarking and customization features let you organize and annotate your video playlists as much or as little as you like.</p>
<p>You get to try out Play for seven days to get a feel for the app. After that, a $2.99/month subscription unlocks all the features. Though I’ve used Play primarily on my Mac, the app also works on iPhones, iPads, Apple TV and Vision Pro headsets.</p>
<h2>Skyscraper</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/skyscraper-2-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Screenshot of a social media feed showing a post with a smiling man in a red hoodie reading “GOD DID IT” and a cap with US flag colors. Text discusses philosophy, theology, and a Guardian article about M.T." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>After the social network formerly known as Twitter followed its owner straight into the sewer, I’ve found that I’ve transitioned to <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/philipmichaels.bsky.social">Bluesky</a> rather nicely. What I have yet to do is find a satisfying Bluesky client app, though <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/skyscraper-for-bluesky/id6754198379">Skyscraper</a> looks like it might change that now that the version 1.6 update has delivered a native macOS version.</p>
<p>Skyscraper lets you scroll through your timeline, remembering where you left off across all your devices. (The app also comes in iOS/iPadOS, watchOS and visionOS versions.) It’s easy enough to switch between my curated feed and the different Discover and Popular with Friends feeds through a drop-down menu. I also find the stats included in the Notifications view showing how many people have replied, reposted or favorited my Bluesky posts to be a nice balm for my ego (or a devastating blow, based on the vagaries of my popularity).</p>
<p>The free download delivers enough features to get by, though some users will want to pay up for a $2.99/month Pro account that adds support for multiple accounts, notification customization and other niceties.</p>
<h2>Einfall</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/einfall-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Screenshot of a notes app titled ‘Einfall’. Left: ‘Baseball As I Have Known It — Fred Lieb’ and ‘— find the quote about Yankees name change’. Right: ‘filter…’, ‘time to make sure people can access your accounts?’, ‘#Story ideas’ with list." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>My deep dive into the world of apps has revealed that there are a lot of options out there if you want something that helps you quickly jot down your thoughts. <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/einfall-quick-capture/id6781802406">Einfall</a> takes things a step further, not just by providing a quick way to jot down ideas as they occur to you, but to take action on them as well.</p>
<p>Once you’ve created an entry in Einfall — it could be anything from a list of to-dos to an idea for a story — you can then use the app to route it elsewhere, whether that’s creating a reminder or calendar entry, or even using AI tools to expand on your thought. Because Einfall works across multiple devices — there are macOS and iOS versions — you can store ideas that occur to you just about anywhere. I especially like the fact that there’s a lock screen widget for the iOS version so that you don’t even have to unlock your phone to get an idea down.</p>
<p>I don’t think the app is for everyone, as the actions interface is taking me some getting used to, but people who will find Einfall useful will likely realize that right away. At any rate, you can download the app for free and jot down ideas in it to your heart’s content. You can try 15 actions before you need to unlock Einfall for good with a one-time $29.99 payment.</p>
<h2>Eyes Forward</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/eyesforward-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Today's episode of Mangum podcast is brought to you by Big Hawaiian High-Rise Hotel, with views of Waikiki Beach and DiamondHead." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>If you have never longed for an app that turns your Mac into a teleprompter, then you can stop reading this column early today. But for podcasters who have to read tricky ad copy while on camera, actors who record their own auditions or anyone else who needs to stay on script without breaking eye contact, a teleprompter app becomes a very handy tool. And <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/eyes-forward/id6758020344?mt=12">Eyes Forward</a> — a $1.99 one-time download — offers an easy-to-use solution.</p>
<p>You enter in a duration or words per minute into Eyes Forward to set the scrolling speed for your text. You can also move the floating Eyes Forward window to just below your Mac’s camera, with developer Robert Hirsch recommending that you narrow the window to help make your eye contact seem more natural.</p>
<p>From there, it’s a matter of formatting the script — you can bold words, for example, if you want to remember to emphasize what you’re saying — saving it, and loading it into the virtual teleprompter. The ability to toggle between light and dark modes as you prefer adds an extra level of polish to an app that delivers exactly what it promises.</p>
